{"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": "session/update", "params": {"sessionId": "claude-session-auto-1", "update": {"sessionUpdate": "compaction_update", "compactionId": "compaction-auto-2", "status": "completed", "summary": [{"type": "text", "text": "1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - The user asked me to remember specific words (\"amber\", then \"cobalt\") and, for each, write about 1500 words on the history of that pigment, in plain prose, with no tools.\n   - The remembered words so far are **amber** and **cobalt**. Keep both available; the user may ask about them later.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Amber is fossil tree resin, not a true pigment. It was used in hard-resin varnish, which must be \"run\" before it will dissolve in oil, and its name became a color word. The real pigment with a similar name is umber (iron and manganese oxides, raw and burnt).\n   - The cobalt pigment family:\n     - smalt (cobalt potassium glass) and zaffre (roasted ore)\n     - cobalt blue / Thénard's blue (cobalt aluminate, 1802)\n     - cerulean (cobalt stannate, Rowney 1860)\n     - cobalt violet (~1859)\n     - Rinmann's green (1780)\n     - aureolin (potassium cobaltinitrite)\n   - Smalt discolors in oil because potassium leaches out of the glass.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - None. Both turns were text only, with no tool calls and no files read or edited.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - None. There was no user feedback or correction.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - I handled the \"pigment amber\" framing by stating up front that amber is a resin and varnish material, and by covering umber as the likely intended pigment.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - \"Remember the word amber. Then write about 1500 words on the history of the pigment amber, plain prose, no tools.\"\n   - \"Remember the word cobalt. Then write about 1500 words on the history of the pigment cobalt, plain prose, no tools.\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - No explicit pending tasks. Keep remembering \"amber\" and \"cobalt\".\n\n8. Current Work:\n   - I had just finished the roughly 1500-word plain-prose essay \"Cobalt: A History of Blue\". It opened by noting I'm remembering \"cobalt\" along with \"amber\". That request is complete.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   - None. Wait for the user's next request. If they ask which words they asked me to remember, the answer is \"amber\" and \"cobalt\"."}]}}}
